1
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:09,906
One job from start to finish: a home library. Bookcases on every wall, a sliding bookcase door at the entry, and a custom table in the middle.

2
00:00:09,906 --> 00:00:15,910
The room: 14' x 12' (168" x 144"), with an 8' (96") ceiling. The entry is a 36" opening in the front wall.

3
00:00:15,910 --> 00:00:22,414
Everything here is done in the app as it is. Where the app can’t do something, a caption says so and shows the workaround.

4
00:00:22,414 --> 00:00:25,438
Start on the Jobs page: New job.

5
00:00:25,438 --> 00:00:36,910
A job number, a name and the customer.

6
00:00:36,910 --> 00:00:44,872
An empty job. The tabs along the top are the whole workflow: cabinets, room, custom parts, cut list, nesting, quote, output.

7
00:00:44,872 --> 00:00:48,674
Room first, so every cabinet has a wall to go on.

8
00:00:48,674 --> 00:00:56,414
Room width is the back wall: 168". Room depth, back wall to front wall: 144".

9
00:00:56,414 --> 00:01:00,615
Wall height: 96", an 8' ceiling. The tall bookcases will stop under it.

10
00:01:00,615 --> 00:01:06,756
Plan view stays empty until there are cabinets. When it fills, the back wall is at the top and the front wall, with the entry, at the bottom.

11
00:01:06,756 --> 00:01:14,262
Honest note: the room has walls and a ceiling height, but no door or window object. The 36" entry will simply be a gap left in the front run.

12
00:01:14,262 --> 00:01:21,278
Snap is on: a cabinet dragged near a wall lines up against it. Hold Alt to place freely. That is how the side and front runs will go in.

13
00:01:21,278 --> 00:01:25,781
The room is set and saved. Next: the cabinets, from the template library.

14
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:33,553
The layout, worked out on paper first. Back wall 168": six bookcases 28" wide. Each side wall: four 30" units.

15
00:01:33,553 --> 00:01:41,258
Front wall: two 30" bookcases left of a 36" entry, then 48" of bare wall where the sliding bookcase door parks when it is open.

16
00:01:41,258 --> 00:01:47,862
Every unit is 12" deep and 90" tall: 6" under the ceiling, room for a crown or a scribe strip on site.

17
00:01:47,862 --> 00:01:58,690
Add cabinet opens the template library. The Tall 24" x 84" pantry is the closest start: tall carcass, shelf-pin rows.

18
00:01:58,690 --> 00:02:20,381
Open it with the pencil and turn it into the back-wall bookcase: 28" wide, 90" tall, 12" deep.

19
00:02:20,381 --> 00:02:27,310
Doors: None. A library wall is open shelving: no hinges, no cup boring.

20
00:02:27,310 --> 00:02:34,447
Six adjustable shelves: seven openings of about 12", right for most books. 28" is a safe span for a 3/4" shelf under books.

21
00:02:34,447 --> 00:02:40,774
Shelf-pin rows on the 32 mm system pitch, front and back, so the shelves can be moved later.

22
00:02:40,774 --> 00:02:47,810
Shelf fronts are what you see in a library, so band them with the 1 mm ABS, not the 0.4 mm tape.

23
00:02:47,810 --> 00:02:57,937
The parts table: two sides, top, bottom, back and six shelves.

24
00:02:57,937 --> 00:03:10,692
The back wall needs six identical units: copy this one five times.

25
00:03:10,692 --> 00:03:15,652
Six bookcases, 28" x 90" x 12". Set one up carefully, then copy: every unit stays identical.

26
00:03:16,867 --> 00:03:21,222
The back wall run. Six units of 28" make exactly 168", wall to wall.

27
00:03:21,222 --> 00:03:26,138
Arrange along the back wall lines the cabinets up in order, from the left corner.

28
00:03:26,138 --> 00:03:35,668
Plan view: six units against the back wall, 12" deep. The thick line on each is its front, facing into the room.

29
00:03:35,668 --> 00:03:45,490
Elevation, back wall: no doors, so every shelf shows. The shelf lines run level across all six units.

30
00:03:45,490 --> 00:03:52,642
90" tall under a 96" ceiling: the 6" gap at the top is left on purpose for a crown or scribe strip fitted on site.

31
00:03:52,642 --> 00:04:09,511
In 3D: the first wall of the library.

32
00:04:09,511 --> 00:04:16,663
Note: the toe kick from the pantry template is kept: a recessed plinth reads well under a library run and protects the bottom shelf.

33
00:04:16,663 --> 00:04:23,267
Arrange only fills the back wall (and its corners). The side walls are placed by hand next, with Rotate and snap.

34
00:04:24,500 --> 00:04:30,404
The side walls: four 30" units on each, 120" between the back run and the front wall. Arrange can’t do side walls, so these go in by hand.

35
00:04:30,404 --> 00:04:40,964
The method: copy a bookcase. The copy lands in the back-left corner, on top of T1.

36
00:04:40,964 --> 00:04:51,340
Drag it into the open room first, then set Width to 30". Order matters: widening a unit that is already turned shifts it off its wall (logged as a bug).

37
00:04:51,340 --> 00:06:23,401
Rotate three times: the thick front edge now faces right, into the room. Then drag it to the left wall; snap pulls it flush.

38
00:06:23,401 --> 00:08:23,652
Right wall: the same steps, but one Rotate turns the front to face left, into the room.

39
00:08:23,652 --> 00:09:41,019
Front wall: two 30" units left of the entry. Two Rotates turn them to face the back wall.

40
00:09:41,019 --> 00:09:47,521
Sixteen units placed. The gap on the front wall, right of the two units, is the 36" entry and the 48" wall where the sliding door will park.

41
00:09:47,521 --> 00:09:54,525
Honest note: there is no typed X/Y position. Most units landed within 1/4", but the last left-wall unit (T10) snapped onto the corner and pokes past the wall. Fixed next.

42
00:09:54,525 --> 00:10:02,691
Check each wall in Elevation. Left wall:

43
00:10:02,691 --> 00:10:08,004
Right wall:

44
00:10:08,004 --> 00:10:14,455
Front wall: two units, then the open stretch for the entry and the door.

45
00:10:14,455 --> 00:10:56,151
And in 3D: bookcases on three walls and part of the fourth.

46
00:11:01,839 --> 00:11:08,461
First, the fix. T10, top of the left wall, snapped onto the corner bookcase. The yellow banner says which units overlap or run past a wall.

47
00:11:08,461 --> 00:11:23,295
Hold Alt and drag it down, flush to the wall and the unit below. Alt turns snap off, so it goes exactly where it is dropped.

48
00:11:23,295 --> 00:11:28,299
T10 is fixed. The banner still lists T15 and T16: the front pair sits 0.3" past the front wall. They get the same Alt-drag nudge next.

49
00:11:28,299 --> 00:11:35,510
Now the front wall. Two 30" units from 12" to 72", then the 36" entry, from 72" to 108".

50
00:11:35,510 --> 00:11:42,478
From 108" to 156": 48" of bare wall. When the sliding bookcase door opens, it rolls right and parks here.

51
00:11:42,478 --> 00:11:49,484
Why 48"? The door will be 40" wide, 2" past the opening on each side, so it needs at least 40" to park clear of the entry.

52
00:11:49,484 --> 00:11:59,603
Elevation, front wall: the two bookcases, then the open wall where the door and its track will go.

53
00:11:59,603 --> 00:12:05,608
Remember: the room has no door object, so the entry is just this gap. A caption will mark it in 3D too.

54
00:12:11,437 --> 00:12:32,757
First the nudge from last time: Alt-drag the two front units up by a hair, so they sit on the front wall, not 0.3" past it.

55
00:12:32,757 --> 00:12:38,762
Now the sliding bookcase door. It covers the 36" entry and rolls right along a wall-mounted track to open.

56
00:12:38,762 --> 00:13:13,420
The bookcase part is a real cabinet: copy a bookcase, drag it clear, then size it: 40" wide (2" past each side of the opening), 84" tall, 10" deep.

57
00:13:13,420 --> 00:13:19,478
Height off the floor 1/2": a hung door must clear the floor. App bug: the field shows 1/2" but a fraction is not saved (1" is). The clearance is set by the rollers on site.

58
00:13:19,478 --> 00:13:37,190
Two Rotates face it into the room, then Alt-drag it over the entry: 70" to 110" along the front wall.

59
00:13:37,190 --> 00:13:46,822
Rename it in the editor so the cut list and labels say what it is.

60
00:13:46,822 --> 00:13:54,325
Honest note: the app has no sliding-door or roller hardware for a cabinet. The rollers bolt to the top of this unit on site, from the track kit.

61
00:13:54,325 --> 00:14:04,474
Its back faces the hallway when closed, so the white HDF back is the finished face outside. A painted panel could be added later.

62
00:14:04,474 --> 00:14:24,070
The track kit itself goes in the Library, under Hardware: Add row.

63
00:14:24,070 --> 00:14:29,212
Price: $320 here is an EXAMPLE price, for the demo only.

64
00:14:29,212 --> 00:14:37,217
Honest limit: a library row can’t be attached to a job, and the Quote has no manual line. So this kit will NOT appear in the job’s quote. Add it by hand.

65
00:14:37,217 --> 00:14:49,623
What the shop does make: a header board the steel track screws to. That is a custom part in the job.

66
00:14:49,623 --> 00:15:08,044
Track header: 96" long, 5-1/2" tall, from 18 mm MDF (paint grade). There is no solid-wood stock in the library.

67
00:15:08,044 --> 00:15:16,602
Screw holes for the track: 3/8" holes every 16", 2-3/4" up, to line up with the track’s own holes. First hole 4" in.

68
00:15:16,602 --> 00:15:45,185
Five more at 20, 36, 52, 68 and 84 inches along: 16" centres, all inside the 96" board.

69
00:15:45,185 --> 00:15:56,636
Select everything (Ctrl+A), then Add operation → Drill and Use selection: the drill takes the six circles as holes.

70
00:15:56,636 --> 00:16:00,331
Cut through: on. The screws pass right through the board into the studs.

71
00:16:00,331 --> 00:16:11,400
The warning is honest: the placeholder tool table has no 3/8" drill. Load the real tools before cutting. That is the shop’s job, not a demo shortcut.

72
00:16:11,400 --> 00:16:18,405
The door: a real cabinet. The header: a real CNC part. The steel track and rollers: bought in, with an example price, outside the quote.

73
00:16:19,633 --> 00:16:25,038
The table in the middle of the room: 60" x 36", 30" high. Every part of it is a custom part, cut on the CNC.

74
00:16:25,038 --> 00:16:34,041
Honest limits first: the thickest stock in the library is 18 mm, so the top is two layers glued up (about 1-7/16"). And custom parts can’t be placed in the room, so the table won’t show in 3D.

75
00:16:34,041 --> 00:16:54,754
Upper layer of the top: New part on the job’s Custom parts tab. 60" x 36", 18 mm MDF.

76
00:16:54,754 --> 00:17:09,078
A Rounded rectangle with 2" corners, typed in: 0,0 then @60,36. Use as part outline.

77
00:17:09,078 --> 00:17:15,516
The old square-cornered rectangle is still there. Click its corner and Delete it.

78
00:17:15,516 --> 00:17:28,858
A monogram for the Hartleys: Text, "H", 6" tall, baseline at 28,15, near the centre.

79
00:17:28,858 --> 00:17:45,143
Machining. Click the H, then Add operation → Engrave: the cutter follows the letter’s lines.

80
00:17:45,143 --> 00:17:58,567
Click empty space so nothing is selected: then an operation uses the outline. Chamfer: a bevel along the top edge, the shaped edge of the table.

81
00:17:58,567 --> 00:18:11,938
And Profile: cut the shape out of the sheet, rounded corners and all.

82
00:18:11,938 --> 00:19:00,984
The lower layer: the same outline, no chamfer or monogram, so it stays hidden under the upper one. Same steps, sped up.

83
00:19:00,984 --> 00:19:38,656
Legs: each leg is two slabs glued into an L, 28-1/2" x 3-1/2". Four legs, eight slabs. 28-1/2" + the top = about 30" high.

84
00:19:38,656 --> 00:20:53,623
Aprons tie the legs together under the top: two long (50") and two short (26"), 4" deep.

85
00:20:53,623 --> 00:21:01,127
The job’s custom parts: the track header, the two top layers, eight leg slabs and four aprons. Joinery (pocket screws or dowels) is done at the bench.

86
00:21:02,333 --> 00:21:13,339
Review time. Elevations first: every wall, square-on, to check heights and shelf lines.

87
00:21:13,339 --> 00:21:21,822
Left and right walls: four 30" units each, shelves level with the back run.

88
00:21:21,822 --> 00:21:29,100
Front wall: two bookcases, then the sliding door: 84" tall, lower than the 90" units, so the track fits above it.

89
00:21:29,100 --> 00:23:23,030
In 3D, from the entry side. The table is not shown: custom parts can’t be placed in the room (logged as a feature idea).

90
00:23:23,030 --> 00:23:34,077
Materials. A library this size looks better in wood tone: Cabinets tab → Change materials.

91
00:23:34,077 --> 00:23:39,523
Carcass: PB18-OAK, the natural oak melamine with grain. (There are no doors, so fronts don’t matter here.)

92
00:23:39,523 --> 00:23:45,124
Carcass front edges in oak ABS too: on open shelving every front edge is on show.

93
00:23:45,124 --> 00:23:56,996
Preview: every part that changes, and the sheets before and after.

94
00:23:56,996 --> 00:24:02,510
Apply to all 17 cabinets.

95
00:24:02,510 --> 00:24:15,907
Check a bookcase: the shelf fronts are still white ABS. Change materials has no shelf-front picker, so each unit would need editing (logged as a feature idea; left white here).

96
00:24:15,907 --> 00:24:22,409
The custom parts stay in MDF: the table and the track header are painted, a different finish from the oak bookcases.

97
00:24:22,409 --> 00:25:02,472
In 3D: the library in oak.

98
00:25:08,837 --> 00:25:16,318
One more catch from review: the banner says T17 and T16 overlap. The 40" door ran 1-5/8" into the bookcase left of the entry.

99
00:25:16,318 --> 00:25:28,957
Fix: Alt-drag the door right so its left edge meets that bookcase at 72". It will cover 72" to 112": the 36" opening plus 4" on the open side.

100
00:25:28,957 --> 00:25:33,460
The overlap warning is gone: the door now clears the bookcase.

101
00:25:33,460 --> 00:25:38,962
On site the track gets a stop at that end, so the door closes against the bookcase and doesn’t rub it.

102
00:25:38,962 --> 00:25:44,384
Now the export check, on the Output tab. It lists everything that would stop a clean cut.

103
00:25:44,384 --> 00:25:52,426
The warnings are the expected ones for this demo shop: placeholder tool table, placeholder machine values. Nothing about the design itself.

104
00:25:52,426 --> 00:26:00,510
Machining output switches stay OFF. Turning them on is for a real shop with real tools, after a woodWOP simulation.

105
00:26:00,510 --> 00:26:10,698
Cut list: every bookcase part, the door, and the custom parts, grouped by material.

106
00:26:10,698 --> 00:26:19,351
The table and the track header, in MDF: top layers, eight leg slabs, four aprons.

107
00:26:19,351 --> 00:26:27,900
Hardware: shelf pins and Confirmat screws from the bookcases. The track kit is not here: library rows can’t be linked to a job.

108
00:26:27,900 --> 00:26:38,132
Nesting packs the parts onto sheets: oak melamine for the bookcases, MDF for the table and header.

109
00:26:38,132 --> 00:26:48,469
The two 60" x 36" top layers and the 96" header fit on one 5' x 12' MDF sheet; the slabs and aprons fill in around them.

110
00:26:48,469 --> 00:26:54,974
Oak sheets are 5' x 12': the 90" sides run along the 144" length, with the grain running up the bookcases.

111
00:26:56,200 --> 00:27:04,031
The quote. Sheets, edgeband, hardware and labour are all counted, but no prices are set yet, so the total says "incomplete".

112
00:27:04,031 --> 00:27:10,033
Every price typed from here on is an EXAMPLE price, for the demo only. Use your own supplier’s prices.

113
00:27:10,033 --> 00:27:37,558
Library → Materials. Sheet goods here are costed per square foot. Example prices: oak melamine $1.60, MDF $1.10, 1/4" HDF back $0.70.

114
00:27:37,558 --> 00:27:46,389
Edgebands, per foot. Example prices: oak ABS $0.30, white ABS $0.25.

115
00:27:46,389 --> 00:27:55,650
Hardware, per piece. Example prices: Confirmat screw $0.08, shelf pin $0.10.

116
00:27:55,650 --> 00:28:04,411
Labour: Settings → Pricing. Example price: $65 an hour. The minutes per cabinet are still the placeholder times; set your own.

117
00:28:04,411 --> 00:28:13,400
Back to the quote.

118
00:28:13,400 --> 00:28:22,115
Every line is priced now. Board, edgeband, hardware, labour, and a total, all at the example prices above.

119
00:28:22,115 --> 00:28:28,621
Not in it, by design of the app: the $320 example track kit. Add it to the customer’s quote by hand.

120
00:28:28,621 --> 00:28:38,228
Labels: one per part, for every bookcase panel and custom part, from the Output tab.

121
00:28:38,228 --> 00:31:01,687
The finished library, in 3D: oak bookcases on every wall, the entry, and the sliding bookcase door over it.

122
00:31:01,687 --> 00:31:55,212
The table stands in the middle in real life; in the app it lives on the Custom parts tab, cut list and nest.

123
00:31:55,212 --> 00:32:01,717
Room, cabinets, door, table, materials, export check, cut list, nest, quote and labels: one job, start to finish.
